diff --git a/docs/RMA_183_MEMORY_STORAGE_PRESSURE_SPEC_2026-08-15.md b/docs/RMA_183_MEMORY_STORAGE_PRESSURE_SPEC_2026-08-15.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..890d491
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/RMA_183_MEMORY_STORAGE_PRESSURE_SPEC_2026-08-15.md
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# RMA-183 Memory and Storage Pressure Specification
+
+Date: 2026-08-15
+
+## Objective
+
+RMA-183 hardens the Android digital-twin runtime against memory and storage pressure without introducing a second resource-priority policy, corrupting active state, or silently deleting durable user data.
+
+The implementation reuses the existing RMA-135 resource snapshot/governor and RMA-181 priority-degradation path. Callback-driven pressure augments those signals and performs bounded resource release; it does not change simulation fidelity, physics timestep, provider fallback policy, or calibrated-state claims.
+
+## Memory-pressure ingress
+
+Two Android/Unity ingress paths are supported:
+
+- `Application.lowMemory` produces a critical pressure signal.
+- On Android, `ReachyAndroidMemoryTrimMonitor` registers `android.content.ComponentCallbacks2` and maps `onLowMemory()` plus `onTrimMemory(int)` into `ReachyMemoryPressureSignal`.
+
+The callback latch `ReachyAndroidMemoryPressureState` only escalates between healthy `ActivityManager.MemoryInfo` snapshots. A pressure latch is cleared only after Android no longer reports low memory and available memory exceeds twice the Android low-memory threshold. Critical callback state is ORed into the existing RMA-135 `LocalLlmResourceSnapshot`; no competing byte threshold is introduced.
+
+Android callbacks may arrive off the Unity main thread. The host therefore only queues/coalesces the strongest pending signal from callbacks. `Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets()` and task/result processing happen from `ReachyApplicationHostBehaviour.Update()` on the Unity thread.
+
+## Coordinated memory release
+
+`ReachyMemoryPressureCoordinator` serializes target execution and records one explicit result per target. A target exception is converted into a visible failure result and does not prevent later targets from attempting cleanup.
+
+The local LLM provider implements `IReachyMemoryPressureTarget`:
+
+- moderate pressure retains the loaded model;
+- critical pressure increments the conversation epoch, cancels active generation, waits for that generation to drain, then unloads the optional native model;
+- the model handle is claimed before crossing the asynchronous unload boundary so concurrent disposal cannot double-unload it;
+- disposal waits for an in-flight pressure release before disposing the owned runtime;
+- a reload already in `Loading` causes critical pressure release to report a visible deferred failure instead of racing the native model lifecycle;
+- after a successful pressure unload, provider state is `Unavailable` and generation cannot silently reload the model; recovery requires an explicit `ReloadAsync` call.
+
+The production application composition still does not invent an unavailable provider stack. Any explicitly composed memory-pressure target participates; the Local LLM target is exercised directly by managed contracts and is ready for the production provider composition when that runtime is installed.
+
+## Model-package storage pressure
+
+RMA-132's manifest-bound package manager remains authoritative for local-model storage. RMA-183 strengthens it in two ways:
+
+1. Initial import/download storage preflight remains unchanged.
+2. `CopyExactAsync` now re-checks free space before every destination write chunk.
+
+For resumable downloads, a mid-stream `InsufficientStorage` result preserves the exact manifest/source-bound `.part` and metadata so a later explicit download can resume from the written offset. Non-resumable import partials are removed on storage failure. Size/hash/finalization checks remain unchanged.
+
+`CleanupTransientStorage()` is non-blocking with respect to package operations. If an import/download/resolve operation owns the package gate, cleanup returns `OperationBusy`. Otherwise it removes only the package manager's staging and quarantine trees using the existing no-follow path rules. Verified installed artifacts are never part of this cleanup operation.
+
+## Diagnostic-export storage pressure
+
+Before RMA-172 writes a diagnostic bundle, `ReachyDiagnosticBundleStorageGate` requires:
+
+- a 16 MiB write budget; and
+- a 64 MiB safety reserve.
+
+The RMA-172 exporter itself caps bundles at 8 MiB, so the RMA-183 write budget intentionally provides headroom above the exporter maximum. If free-space probing fails or headroom is insufficient, export fails before a file is created and records a storage-preflight diagnostic.
+
+## Cleanup UI
+
+The Local Model settings panel exposes `CLEAN APP STORAGE`. The operation is deliberately narrow:
+
+- remove transient local-model staging/quarantine content only when the exact managed-store ownership marker exists;
+- retain every verified installed local model;
+- remove old `reachy-diagnostics-*.zip` files while preserving the newest three bundles;
+- ignore diagnostic reparse points;
+- return bytes freed, entry count, and a visible success/failure message.
+
+The cleanup control is not a general recursive delete and does not touch conversation history, settings, credentials, calibrated assets, or arbitrary files beneath `persistentDataPath`.
+
+## Fail-closed invariants
+
+- Memory pressure never changes or skips authoritative physics steps.
+- No cloud provider is selected as a memory-pressure fallback.
+- A failed native LLM unload faults the provider rather than claiming memory was released.
+- Storage probe failure is treated as insufficient/unknown capacity, not as permission to write.
+- Installed verified models are preserved by user cleanup.
+- Mid-download low storage never finalizes or hashes a truncated artifact as installed.
+- Diagnostic export does not start when its storage budget cannot be guaranteed.
+
+## Validation boundary
+
+Local validation covers source contracts, deterministic managed test sources, package-manager behavior, storage policy, and repository static checks. This environment has no .NET SDK, Unity Editor, or attached Android device, so RMA-183 does not claim a local managed build or physical `onTrimMemory`/low-storage run. The publication gate must run warnings-as-errors managed projects on GitHub before the integration can fast-forward `master`.
